WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.300
A shark movie without any bite? "Meg 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.920
The Trench" isn't making a splash with Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes critics for some surprising reasons.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:13.380
In 2018, director Jon Turteltaub surprised&nbsp;
audiences with his kinetic and intriguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.580
"The Meg." Based on Steve Alten's novel of&nbsp;
the same name, the film follows a megalodon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.080
that pesters a group of scientists and&nbsp;
a very exhausted Jason Statham. While it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.920
was far from perfect, "The Meg" was good&nbsp;
summer fun, giving viewers a compelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.120
action-heavy journey through the depths&nbsp;
of the ocean. In a landscape littered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.680
superhero flicks and reboots, "The Meg" was a&nbsp;
breath of fresh blockbuster air that featured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.420
an all-star cast and a competent narrative&nbsp;
about big sharks. What else could anyone want?

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.620
Audiences around the world were receptive&nbsp;
to Turteltaub's nightmarish shark flick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:49.860
helping "The Meg" gross over $500 million at&nbsp;
the global box office. After proving itself as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.840
global financial success, it wasn't surprising to&nbsp;
hear that Warner Bros. was interested in a sequel.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.440
"Jonas, we've got nothing left."
"Almost nothing."

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.240
Ben Wheatley, the auteur behind "Free Fire"&nbsp;
and "Kill List," was brought on board to helm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.160
the sequel. Seeing as Wheatley had never helmed a&nbsp;
large, big-budget blockbuster before, having the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.780
British director steer the "Meg 2: The Trench"&nbsp;
ship felt like an inspiring decision. Could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:18.300
Wheatley turn a potentially bloated sequel into a&nbsp;
genuine, pulse-pounding work of deep sea horror?

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.840
Eh, doesn't look like it. Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes critics were the first to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.680
see Wheatley's "Meg 2: The Trench"&nbsp;
and they're not very enthusiastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.120
about it. IGN critic Matt Donato was not&nbsp;
impressed with the film at all, writing:

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.880
"'Meg 2: The Trench' has all the excitement of&nbsp;
fishing solo for two hours without a single bite."

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.840
Ouch. Rotten Tomatoes critics were&nbsp;
surprisingly disappointed with how "Meg 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.740
The Trench" is occupied with elevating&nbsp;
itself instead of fully leaning into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.740
its insane premise. Graeme Tuckett of Stuff wrote:

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.840
"'Meg 2' is burdened with an actual plot. [...]&nbsp;
The sharks barely show up. And when they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.640
they are such clearly CGI creations, it really&nbsp;
does look as though Statham and chums are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.540
attacked by characters from an animation&nbsp;
that have stumbled into the wrong film."

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.260
Deadline wasn't impressed with the&nbsp;
sci-fi aquatic sequel either, saying,

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.980
"At several points along the way you could&nbsp;
bang your hand to your forehead in reaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.420
to how random and non-transporting the action&nbsp;
is, even when there's a great deal of it."

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.600
"This was a bad idea."
"Just a little bit."

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.920
Well, a movie about giant sharks certainly&nbsp;
won't appeal to everyone. Is the film fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.600
at least? CinemaBlend's Eric Eisenberg was&nbsp;
particularly disappointed in the film, saying,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.860
"'Meg 2: The Trench' wants to just be&nbsp;
seen as dumb fun, but it doesn't earn it."

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.520
Clearly, most of the critics who took to&nbsp;
Rotten Tomatoes before the film's release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.260
weren't fans of "Meg 2." Some critics went&nbsp;
ahead and even labeled the film as boring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.020
perhaps the worst assessment&nbsp;
any project can receive.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:45.180
But despite the mediocre reviews, "Meg 2: The&nbsp;
Trench" still has some fans in its sunken corner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.840
Calum Marsh of The New York Times had a&nbsp;
few positive sentiments about the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.400
saying that the sequel features a "a spark of&nbsp;
wit" that fits in effortlessly with its narrative.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.260
Katie Walsh of the St. Louis Post Dispatch&nbsp;
also acknowledged the film's shortcomings but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.920
couldn't help but come around to Wheatley's&nbsp;
chaotic vision as it wrapped up, writing:

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.780
"About halfway through 'Meg 2: The Trench,'&nbsp;
this self-serious sequel suddenly becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.260
funny, [...] leaning into the slippery&nbsp;
silliness of a summer shark flick."

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.840
Liz Shannon Miller of Consequence put it bluntly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.040
reminding viewers that this is ultimately&nbsp;
a movie about giant sharks, saying:

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.580
"['Meg 2: The Trench' is] a far more&nbsp;
coherent entry in the genre than others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.280
with effects work that's&nbsp;
several notches above the rest."

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.640
While most Rotten Tomatoes critics&nbsp;
are sour on the "Meg" sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.300
there are a few that find the film&nbsp;
compelling enough. Will these mixed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.540
negative reviews impact the film's&nbsp;
box office? Only time will tell.

